There is a moment many mothers know. You are looking at your baby's face at four in the morning, and you feel a love so big it almost hurts. And in the same breath you think: I cannot do one more night like this.

Then the guilt comes. How can I feel this tired of someone I love this much?

I would like to take that guilt off your shoulders, even if only for a few minutes.

## Both things are true

Love and exhaustion are not opposites. They live in the same body, often in the same hour. Feeling worn out does not mean you love your baby less. It means you are a person, and people need sleep, food and rest to feel like themselves.

Postpartum can feel tender, beautiful, confusing and exhausting all at once. Nobody gets to keep only the beautiful parts.

Somewhere along the way, many of us learned that a good mother finds every moment magical. That idea puts so much weight on women who are already carrying so much.

You can be grateful and tired. You can adore your baby and still miss your old sleep, your old body, your quiet mornings. Missing those things is not a betrayal. It is grief for a life that changed, and it can sit right next to joy.

## When tiredness is more than tiredness

Almost every new parent is tired. That is part of the season. But sometimes what looks like exhaustion is something that needs more care.

If you feel hopeless, if you cannot sleep even when the baby is sleeping, if you feel numb, very anxious or disconnected from your baby, or if you have thoughts that scare you, please do not keep it to yourself. Tell your OB, midwife or doctor. These feelings are more common than people talk about, and there is real help for them.

Reaching out does not mean you love your baby less. It is a way of caring for both of you.

## What can help a little

I will not tell you to "sleep when the baby sleeps" and leave it there. You have heard that one. Here are a few smaller things that often make a real difference.

**Protect one stretch of sleep.** Even one longer block, with someone else caring for the baby, can change how the next day feels.

**Lower the bar on everything that is not you or the baby.** The laundry can wait. The thank you notes can wait. The house does not need to look ready for visitors.

**Keep food and water close.** A snack and a full bottle of water wherever you usually feed the baby, so you are not choosing between the baby and yourself.

**Say it out loud to someone.** "I love this baby and I am so tired." You might be surprised how many people answer, "Me too."

**Let someone help with the nights, if you can.** A partner, a family member or a postpartum doula. Rest is not a reward you earn at the end. It is part of recovery.

You are not doing it wrong. You are doing something very hard, with a very full heart.

Both of those things are true. And both of them deserve care.
